Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 3 useful new finds

This run screened 25+ candidates across pet face geometry, animal
animation, Live2D/2.5D rigging, WebAR, POD automation, mockup generation,
browser matting and caption/video tooling, then removed projects already
surfaced in earlier runs. Three are strong enough to retain.
1. Plaaasma/printify-mcp =E2=80=94 REUSE/COPY PRODUCTION GUARDS

URL: https://github.com/Plaaasma/printify-mcp
Creator: Plaaasma; fork of TSavo/printify-mcp
Activity: current surfaced repository; 1 commit, 1 star, 0 forks
License: ISC

This is much more useful than another generic Printify wrapper because the
author extended the upstream MCP after actually hitting production failure
modes. It adds cost-based pricing, duplicate-title guards, order history,
print placement, product-category correction, mockup-derived
carousels/video ads, and a create-and-publish pipeline.

The key Roast.pet value is the documented failure knowledge:

   - publishing an already-published Printify product can create a second
   orphaned channel listing; this fork refuses unless republish is explicit=
;
   - external.id is not reliably a Shopify product ID immediately after
   publish;
   - upstream list_products had page/limit arguments reversed and could
   silently return an empty page;
   - bad API keys could surface fake-success behavior upstream;
   - update_product could report success while Printify ignored camelCase
   variant keys;
   - publishing requires all seven flags rather than a partial payload.

Install/API route: Node 18+, npm install/build; runs as an MCP server. Only
PRINTIFY_API_KEY is required for core Printify operations; ffmpeg is needed
for video ads.
Dependencies/hardware: Node; ffmpeg for ad video; no GPU needed for the
production/commerce path.
Evidence: the README explicitly documents production-discovered bugs and
the corresponding fixes/guards.
Work replaced: potentially most of our defensive Printify adapter, pricing
logic, duplicate-publish protection and simple product creative generation.
Integration difficulty: LOW.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because Printify behavior/API can change.
Exact experiment: point it at a disposable/unpublished Printify test
product, run create =E2=86=92 pricing =E2=86=92 mockups =E2=86=92 publish g=
uard twice, and verify
the second publish is blocked and all variant prices actually changed.
Verdict: REUSE NOW / COPY PRODUCTION GUARDS.
2. CAFM =E2=80=94 3D cat-like face prior from only 29 stars

URL: https://github.com/sunyifan2017/CAFM-A-3D-Morphable-Model-for-Animals
Creator: sunyifan2017 / research implementation of CAFM
Activity: 12 commits; older research project associated with WACVW 2020
License: license/provenance must be verified before commercial use; do not
ship until checked.

CAFM is a 3D Morphable Model specifically for cat-like animal faces. It was
constructed from 50 3D samples spanning domestic cats and larger felids.
The repo contains a mean face, PCA shape basis, eigenvalues, mesh topology
and image-matching code. The associated data includes 15 facial landmarks,
projection parameters, rotations, scale and translation; the mesh has
roughly 1,256 vertices.

Why this is interesting for Roast.pet: nearly everything we have found so
far treats the pet as 2D landmarks or a generated raster. CAFM offers a
cheap geometric prior for estimating head pose and plausible 3D facial
shape from a cat photo. We do NOT need photorealistic 3D rendering. A
fitted coarse mesh could give us deterministic yaw/pitch, mouth-plane
orientation, eye plane, muzzle depth and perspective-correct placement for
2D mouth/eye overlays.

Install/API route: Python/NumPy; run matching_algorithm.py after obtaining
its face3d dependency. Shape is generated as meanFace + PCA basis =C3=97
coefficients.
Dependencies/hardware: CPU/NumPy for the morphable model fitting path; no
modern GPU requirement inherent in the representation.
Evidence: published WACVW paper, released mean face/PCA/mesh and matching
implementation.
Work replaced: potentially custom cat head-pose heuristics and some
perspective-correction code.
Integration difficulty: MEDIUM-HIGH because it is old research code and
only cat-like animals.
Maintenance risk: HIGH as a dependency, LOW as a mathematical mechanism.
Exact experiment: take 25 front/three-quarter cat customer photos, fit
CAFM, and test whether projected mouth/eye planes improve overlay placement
compared with simple 2D landmark similarity transforms. Do not spend time
modernizing it unless that benchmark wins.
Verdict: COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK; verify commercial licensing first.
3. bssm-oss/image2live2d =E2=80=94 WATCH / COPY RUNTIME-VALIDATION + AUTO-R=
IG
CONTRACT

URL: https://github.com/bssm-oss/image2live2d
Creator: bssm-oss
Activity: surfaced today; 13 commits
License: not sufficiently established from surfaced repository metadata for
commercial copying; verify before source reuse.

This is a very new R&D repo trying to turn a single image into a Live2D
runtime model. Importantly, it is unusually honest about what works: it can
generate a real runtime-loadable static .moc3 bundle from a PNG and a
heuristic multi-part auto-rig with ArtMeshes, warp deformers, expression
parameters, idle motion, physics, hit areas and LipSync/EyeBlink groups,
but it explicitly does NOT claim commercial-quality automatic semantic
segmentation or rigging.

The valuable Roast.pet mechanism is its capability classification and smoke
testing. Outputs are classified as demo_fixture,
contract_bundle_shape_validated, real_cubism_export_attempted,
real_cubism_export_validated or real_runtime_loaded. It actively prevents a
fake fixture or malformed .moc3 from being treated as a successful
generated rig.

That is exactly how Roast.pet should treat generated media capabilities:
every artifact should carry a machine-verifiable capability/provenance
status rather than a vague =E2=80=98success=E2=80=99.

A second useful experiment is whether its heuristic auto-rig can operate on
our already-clean canonical transparent pet, where the problem is easier
than arbitrary anime character decomposition. If a pet can get cheap idle
deformation, blink/lip groups and physics from a deterministic rig, we
might avoid generated video for many AR reactions.

Install/API route: Python CLI plus Live2D Cubism Core for runtime smoke
verification; includes web preview, adapter contracts, tests and smoke
scripts.
Dependencies/hardware: CPU-oriented orchestration/runtime verification; no
generative GPU required for the static/heuristic rig proof itself.
Evidence: it writes real .moc3 bundles and checks that Cubism Core can load
them; the repo explicitly distinguishes runtime-loadable proof from
commercial-quality rigging.
Work replaced: potentially rig bundle plumbing, runtime validation and some
idle/lip/blink scaffolding.
Integration difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: HIGH because it is extremely new and heuristic.
Exact experiment: feed three approved transparent pets=E2=80=94front-facing=
 dog,
long-haired dog, cat=E2=80=94into its heuristic auto-rig path. Ignore artis=
tic
quality initially. Test only: runtime load, blink group, lip-sync group,
subtle idle motion, and whether identity remains exact because the source
texture is preserved.
Verdict: WATCH / COPY VALIDATION CONTRACT / BENCHMARK AUTO-RIG.
TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

   1. Defensive Printify publish logic before testing Plaaasma=E2=80=99s
   production-hardened fork.
   2. Cost/margin pricing and duplicate-publish protection for POD products=
.
   3. Cat head-pose/perspective geometry from scratch before benchmarking a
   real 3D cat morphable model.
   4. A Live2D bundle validator: image2live2d already demonstrates runtime
   smoke tests and explicit capability classes.
   5. Generated-video idle motion for every pet before testing whether a
   deterministic texture-preserving micro-rig is good enough.

Most surprising hobby project

CAFM. A 29-star repository contains an actual parametric 3D cat-like face
model, PCA basis, mesh and image-fitting machinery. It is old, but it
attacks a pet-specific geometry problem that generic avatar stacks largely
ignore.
Fastest integration win

Plaaasma/printify-mcp. The value is not another API wrapper; it is the
accumulated production failure guards. We can test its duplicate-publish
protection and variant-price correctness on one disposable product
immediately.
One repo/author worth following

Plaaasma/printify-mcp. A one-star fork documenting concrete upstream
failure cases is exactly the kind of weird, low-popularity production
knowledge this scanner is supposed to find.
Architecture update

The new useful split is becoming: canonical pet texture/geometry is
durable; animation and commerce adapters are replaceable. For cats, test
whether coarse 3D geometry can improve deterministic overlay/pose
placement. For animation, require machine-verifiable runtime/capability
status. For POD, reuse production-hardened guards rather than discovering
Printify=E2=80=99s edge cases with live customer orders.

